questions.a:
A **composite** is a multiphase material made by combining two or more distinct materials that remain separate (with an interface) in the final structure, giving a combination of properties neither has alone.

**Matrix** (continuous phase; for example epoxy, polyester, metal, ceramic):
- binds the reinforcement together and fixes its position;
- transfers and distributes the applied load to the reinforcement;
- protects the fibres from abrasion, moisture and chemicals;
- gives the shape, surface finish and toughness.

**Reinforcement** (dispersed phase; for example glass, carbon or aramid fibres, particles):
- carries most of the load;
- provides high strength and stiffness;
- can be oriented to give strength in the required direction.

**Advantages**:
- very high strength-to-weight and stiffness-to-weight ratios;
- excellent corrosion and chemical resistance;
- good fatigue and impact resistance;
- low thermal expansion; properties can be tailored by fibre type and orientation;
- parts with complex shapes can be moulded in one piece.

**Applications**: aircraft and helicopter components, satellites; car body panels, leaf springs; boat and ship hulls; wind-turbine blades; pressure vessels and chemical tanks; sports goods (rackets, cricket bat handles, bicycles); bulletproof vests (Kevlar); printed circuit boards (glass/epoxy).

**PPC** is made by intergrinding OPC clinker with about 15-35% pozzolanic material (fly ash, calcined clay, volcanic ash) plus gypsum. A pozzolana has reactive silica (and alumina) that does not bind by itself but reacts with the $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$ released by cement hydration to form extra C-S-H gel.

**Advantages**:
- lower heat of hydration, so suitable for mass concrete;
- better resistance to sulphates, sea water and alkali-aggregate reaction, because less free $\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$ is present;
- denser, less permeable concrete with higher long-term strength;
- uses industrial waste (fly ash), so it is cheaper and gives lower $\mathrm{CO_2}$ per tonne.

Its drawback is slower early strength gain, so it needs longer curing.

topics.gist:
**Galvanic (bimetallic) corrosion** occurs when two **dissimilar metals** are in electrical contact in an electrolyte. The more active metal, higher in the galvanic series (lower potential), becomes the **anode** and corrodes faster than it would alone. The nobler metal becomes the cathode and is protected. Examples:
- steel bolts in a copper or brass sheet;
- copper pipes joined to galvanised steel pipes;
- a zinc-iron couple, where zinc corrodes.

The rate rises with the potential difference and, importantly, with the **cathode/anode area ratio**. A small anode with a large cathode gives intense attack. Prevention:
- choose metals close together in the galvanic series;
- insulate the joint (plastic washers, gaskets);
- keep the anode area large;
- paint the cathode, not the anode;
- use cathodic protection.

**Concentration cell corrosion** occurs on a **single metal** when different parts of it are exposed to electrolytes of different concentration or aeration.
- **Differential aeration** (most common): the part with **less oxygen becomes the anode** and corrodes, and the well-aerated part becomes the cathode. Example: a zinc or iron rod partly immersed in salt water corrodes just below the waterline, while the part near the surface is cathodic. Crevices, the areas under dirt or drops, and buried sections of pipes behave the same way.
- **Metal-ion concentration cell**: the region in contact with the dilute solution of its own ions becomes the anode.

The EMF of a concentration cell follows the Nernst equation.

questions.a:
**Intergranular corrosion** is selective attack along the **grain boundaries** of a metal or alloy, with the grains themselves hardly affected. The metal loses strength and may crumble.

**Cause in stainless steel (sensitisation)**: when austenitic stainless steel (for example 18/8) is heated or slowly cooled through about 500-800 °C, as in the heat-affected zone next to a weld, carbon diffuses to the grain boundaries and forms **chromium carbide** ($\mathrm{Cr_{23}C_6}$). The region next to each boundary is depleted of Cr (below about 12%), cannot stay passive, and becomes **anodic** to the grain interior. It corrodes in a corrosive medium: **weld decay**.

**Prevention**:
- use **low-carbon grades** (304L, 316L, C below 0.03%);
- use **stabilised steels** with Ti or Nb (321, 347), which tie up carbon as TiC/NbC;
- **solution anneal** (heat to about 1050-1100 °C and quench) to redissolve the carbides;
- control welding heat input.

topics.gist:
Each method attacks one part of the corrosion cell.

1. **Proper design and material selection**:
   - avoid contact of dissimilar metals, or insulate them;
   - keep the anode area large;
   - avoid crevices, sharp corners and places where water stagnates (allow drainage);
   - use welded rather than riveted joints;
   - choose pure metals or corrosion-resistant alloys;
   - stress-relieve after fabrication.
2. **Cathodic protection**: make the whole structure the **cathode**, so that it cannot corrode.
   - **Sacrificial anode**: connect a more active metal (Mg, Zn, Al alloys) to the structure. The anode corrodes instead and is replaced periodically. Used for buried pipelines, ship hulls, geysers.
   - **Impressed current**: a DC source drives current from an inert anode (graphite, high-silicon iron, platinised Ti, scrap iron), buried in a **backfill** of coke, gypsum and bentonite, to the structure. Used for long pipelines, tanks and offshore platforms.
3. **Modifying the environment**:
   - **deaeration** (removing $\mathrm{O_2}$);
   - **deactivation** with $\mathrm{Na_2SO_3}$ or $\mathrm{N_2H_4}$, which react with $\mathrm{O_2}$;
   - **dehumidification** with silica gel or alumina;
   - neutralising acids with $\mathrm{NH_3}$, lime or amines.
4. **Corrosion inhibitors**:
   - **Anodic inhibitors** (chromates, phosphates, tungstates, nitrites) form a film at anodic sites. Under-dosing causes pitting.
   - **Cathodic inhibitors**: in acid, organic amines, mercaptans and heavy-metal salts slow $\mathrm{H_2}$ evolution; in neutral media, $\mathrm{Na_2SO_3}$ or $\mathrm{N_2H_4}$ remove $\mathrm{O_2}$, and Mg, Zn or Ni salts precipitate insoluble hydroxides on cathodic areas.
   - **Vapour-phase inhibitors** protect packed machinery.
5. **Protective coatings**: metallic, inorganic (anodising, phosphating) and organic (paints).
